media: cadence: csi2rx, ti: j721e-csi2rx: V4L2 sequence from the sensor's frame numbers

The TI capture driver numbered buffers as it completed them, so a frame lost
between the sensor and the DMA left no hole in the V4L2 sequence (the driver
carried a TODO about this). The Cadence CSI2RX bridge has a per-stream frame
monitor that stores the frame number of the last CSI-2 Frame Start short
packet (STREAMn_MONITOR_CTRL at stream base + 0x10, STREAMn_MONITOR_FRAME at
+ 0x14; AM62A TRM SPRUJ16C 14.9.6.1.2.51/52, same IP as J722S).

cadence: point each stream's frame monitor at the lowest virtual channel routed
to it when the stream starts, clear it when the stream stops, and export
cdns_csi2rx_get_frame_nb() beside cdns_csi2rx_negotiate_ppc().

ti: derive the sequence from those frame numbers once the sensor is seen to
change them, wrap included; keep the buffer count for sensors that send a
constant number. Reading after DMA completion can race the next Frame Start
during vertical blanking: a buffer then reads one high and the next repeats
the number, so the sequence stutters once instead of drifting.

Against ti-linux-6.12.y. Bench check: the frame number a given sensor sends
(IMX296 unknown), and whether a stream monitor tracks a VC that another
stream carries.

